module std.contract_identity

import std.types { Bool, NonEmptyStr }

// ONE CANONICAL CONTRACT IDENTITY, because DESIGN §3 makes a name a semantic carrier: within one
// naming surface and one declared effective version or epoch, holding the name constant may not
// silently change the obligations, quality floor, refusal behavior, billing consequence or remedy.
// A materially different contract needs a materially different name -- and where the name is
// deliberately held constant across a material change, an EPOCH is the only thing that can
// distinguish the two meanings. Without an epoch component a contract identity cannot express the
// difference between "the same contract" and "the same spelling", which is the meaning-fork half of
// §3 in its exact form.
//
// THE IDENTITY IS THE TRIPLE AND NOTHING ELSE. It names WHICH CONTRACT is in question, never what
// that contract does internally. The rule is inherited rather than invented: the fleet's Required CI
// identity already records that a phases_run field would make every phase added to the floor a
// breaking change to fleet admission, which is backwards -- the roster is the contract's own
// business and its evidence, not its consumer's precondition. Generalising that identity here keeps
// that law and adds the one component it was missing.
//
// WHY THE COMPONENTS ARE BRANDED STRINGS IN std RATHER THAN THREE TYPE PARAMETERS. A naming surface,
// a name and an epoch are identity spellings in every domain that has them, so parameterising all
// three would buy no realized variation and would force every consumer to plumb three comparators
// through every equality site (the std.key_relation shape). One equality law, stated once here, is
// what makes "field equality over exactly the three identity components" checkable by reading rather
// than by auditing each caller's comparators. The SUBJECT stays a type parameter, so two domains'
// identities cannot be compared or substituted even though their components share a representation.
type ContractNamingSurface = NonEmptyStr where brand("ContractNamingSurface")
type ContractName = NonEmptyStr where brand("ContractName")

// THE EPOCH IS DISCRETE IDENTITY, NOT A QUANTITY (reviewing-authority ruling, 2026-09-01). It is
// opaque: not ordered, not a semantic version, and nothing arithmetic is performed on it. Giving it
// a numeric or semver grammar would add interpretation no consumer needs, and would create decode
// failures that cannot occur. Consequently a reader of an epoch carried in a text container is
// TOTAL once the container yields a nonempty string -- absent, duplicated and malformed are
// container facts, and there is deliberately NO undecodable arm, because after a nonempty string
// there is no second decoding step that could refuse. Such an arm would be a permanently unreachable
// RED, which §4b forbids at the top rung: a check whose failure is unauthorable carries no
// information and would later be cited as coverage.
type ContractEpoch = NonEmptyStr where brand("ContractEpoch")

// Subject is a phantom: it is the contract this identity is FOR, so a Required CI identity and any
// other domain's identity are different types even though both are surface/name/epoch triples.
type ContractIdentity<Subject> {
  surface: ContractNamingSurface
  name: ContractName
  epoch: ContractEpoch
}

fn contract_identity_eq<Subject>(
  left: ContractIdentity<Subject>,
  right: ContractIdentity<Subject>,
) -> Bool {
  (left.surface as NonEmptyStr) == (right.surface as NonEmptyStr)
    && (left.name as NonEmptyStr) == (right.name as NonEmptyStr)
    && (left.epoch as NonEmptyStr) == (right.epoch as NonEmptyStr)
}

// THE THREE COMPONENT-WISE ANSWERS ARE SEPARATE BECAUSE THEIR REMEDIES ARE SEPARATE. A consumer that
// learns only "not equal" cannot tell reading the wrong producer from reading a different repository
// from reading a contract whose obligations were deliberately revised, and those are three different
// operator actions. This module supplies the component predicates; naming the REFUSAL is the
// consumer's business, because the cause vocabulary belongs to the boundary that refuses. No arm
// here projects a Bool over the three -- that is the no-Bool-projection rule the floor receipt
// authority already established for exactly this shape.
fn contract_surface_eq<Subject>(
  left: ContractIdentity<Subject>,
  right: ContractIdentity<Subject>,
) -> Bool {
  (left.surface as NonEmptyStr) == (right.surface as NonEmptyStr)
}

fn contract_name_eq<Subject>(
  left: ContractIdentity<Subject>,
  right: ContractIdentity<Subject>,
) -> Bool {
  (left.name as NonEmptyStr) == (right.name as NonEmptyStr)
}

fn contract_epoch_eq<Subject>(
  left: ContractIdentity<Subject>,
  right: ContractIdentity<Subject>,
) -> Bool {
  (left.epoch as NonEmptyStr) == (right.epoch as NonEmptyStr)
}
